.\" IOHIDUserDevice(3) — NextBSD virtual HID devices.
.\"
.\" NextBSD-original. Apple documents this API only in the SDK header
.\" comments, so there is no upstream page to vendor.
.\"
.Dd November 1, 2026
.Dt IOHIDUSERDEVICE 3
.Os NextBSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceCreateWithProperties ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceSetDispatchQueue ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceSetCancelHandler ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterGetReportBlock ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterSetReportBlock ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceActivate ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceCancel ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceHandleReportWithTimeStamp ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceHandleReport ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceCopyProperty ,
.Nm IOHIDUserDeviceSetProperty
.Nd HID devices created from userland
.Sh LIBRARY
IOKit Library (libIOKit, \-lIOKit)
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.In IOKit/hid/IOHIDUserDevice.h
.Ft IOHIDUserDeviceRef
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceCreateWithProperties "CFAllocatorRef allocator" "CFDictionaryRef properties" "IOOptionBits options"
.Ft void
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceSetDispatchQueue "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "dispatch_queue_t queue"
.Ft void
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceSetCancelHandler "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "dispatch_block_t handler"
.Ft void
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterGetReportBlock "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "IOHIDUserDeviceGetReportBlock block"
.Ft void
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterSetReportBlock "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "IOHIDUserDeviceSetReportBlock block"
.Ft void
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceActivate "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device"
.Ft void
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceCancel "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device"
.Ft IOReturn
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceHandleReportWithTimeStamp "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "uint64_t timestamp" "const uint8_t *report" "CFIndex reportLength"
.Ft IOReturn
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceHandleReport "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "const uint8_t *report" "CFIndex reportLength"
.Ft CFTypeRef
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceCopyProperty "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "CFStringRef key"
.Ft Boolean
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceSetProperty "IOHIDUserDeviceRef device" "CFStringRef key" "CFTypeRef property"
.Sh DESCRIPTION
An
.Vt IOHIDUserDeviceRef
is a HID device whose reports come from the process that created it.
The kernel's HID drivers see an ordinary hidbus device:
.Xr hkbd 4 ,
.Xr hms 4
and
.Xr hmt 4
attach to it as they would to a USB device,
and the IORegistry shows it as
.Ql IOHIDUserDevice .
.Xr bluetoothd 8
uses it for Bluetooth keyboards, mice and media keys.
The API is Apple's, from
.In IOKit/hid/IOHIDUserDevice.h .
.Pp
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceCreateWithProperties
describes the device with a dictionary keyed by the
.In IOKit/hid/IOHIDKeys.h
names:
.Bl -tag -width "kIOHIDReportDescriptorKey"
.It Dv kIOHIDReportDescriptorKey
The report descriptor, as
.Vt CFData ,
at most 4096 bytes.
Required.
.It Dv kIOHIDVendorIDKey , Dv kIOHIDProductIDKey , Dv kIOHIDVersionNumberKey , Dv kIOHIDCountryCodeKey
Numbers.
.It Dv kIOHIDProductKey , Dv kIOHIDSerialNumberKey
Strings.
A Bluetooth device's serial number is its address.
.It Dv kIOHIDTransportKey
.Dq Bluetooth
or
.Dq BluetoothLowEnergy
makes a Bluetooth HID device; any other value makes a virtual one.
.It Dv kIOHIDRequestTimeoutKey
How long, in milliseconds, the kernel waits for a get- or set-report
answer.
The default is 1000.
.El
.Pp
Other keys are kept by the object and returned by
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceCopyProperty .
.Fa options
is reserved and should be 0.
The first create loads
.Pa IOHIDUserDevice.kext
through
.Xr kextd 8
if
.Pa /dev/hidresource
is missing.
.Pp
The device is published at
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceActivate ,
and the drivers ask for reports while they attach,
so set the queue and register the handlers first.
The get handler fills
.Fa report
and sets
.Fa *reportLength ;
the set handler also receives output reports, such as keyboard LEDs, as
.Dv kIOHIDReportTypeOutput .
Reports begin with the report ID when the descriptor numbers its reports.
Handlers run on the device's queue.
The callback forms
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterGetReportCallback ,
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterGetReportWithReturnLengthCallback
and
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceRegisterSetReportCallback
are also provided.
.Pp
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceHandleReportWithTimeStamp
delivers one input report and may be called from any thread.
A
.Fa timestamp
of 0 means now.
.Pp
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceCancel
removes the device.
The cancel handler runs on the device's queue once no other handler can
run, and is the place to release the device.
Exiting or closing the device has the same effect on the kernel side:
the device's lifetime is the creating process's.
.Sh RETURN VALUES
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceCreateWithProperties
returns
.Dv NULL
if the properties have no usable report descriptor or
.Pa /dev/hidresource
cannot be opened.
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceHandleReportWithTimeStamp
returns
.Dv kIOReturnSuccess ,
.Dv kIOReturnNotReady
before activation,
.Dv kIOReturnOffline
after cancellation, or another
.Vt IOReturn
for a failed write.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width "/dev/hidresource" -compact
.It Pa /dev/hidresource
the virtual HID transport, one open per device
.El
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr hidbus 4 ,
.Xr bluetoothd 8 ,
.Xr ioreg 8 ,
.Xr kextd 8
.Sh CAVEATS
Creating a device needs root.
There is no run-loop scheduling.
.Fn IOHIDUserDeviceSetProperty
only works before activation.
Kernel set-protocol and set-idle requests are answered with success.
